IMPORT prelude

-- Workplace Safety and Health Act 2006 (Singapore) -- who counts as an employee (s 6(2), (3)), who is outside the Act (s 3(2),
-- s 62(1) and Sixth Schedule), the principal's duty to contractors (ss 14(2), 14A(4)), protection of employees (s 18), the
-- scene of a fatal accident (s 25), remedial and stop-work orders and appeals (ss 21, 22), the onus on the accused and on
-- officers (ss 47, 48), the penalties (ss 15, 18, 21, 23, 25, 26, 46, 50, 51, 53), composition (s 56) and civil liability (s 60).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/WSHA2006.txt ("version in
-- force from 1/12/2025"), with amendments to Act 30 of 2024 (platform workers, in force 1 January 2025), Act 12 of 2020 (s 19(3),
-- in force 1 October 2025) and S 754/2025 (in force 1 December 2025) shown.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 9 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository. SCOPED to what an employer, principal, worker
-- or company officer meets: the reach of the Act, the protections in s 18, the orders the Commissioner can make, and the
-- maximum penalties. Not encoded: the content of the general duties in ss 11-17 (they are "so far as is reasonably
-- practicable" standards, not tests), platform-operator duties beyond their penalty, Part 7 management arrangements (officers,
-- committees, auditors -- left to regulations), the Council and codes of practice, inspectors' powers, factories registration
-- beyond s 46, and the Schedules of dangerous occurrences, occupational diseases and machinery. Which offences are compoundable,
-- and what must be notified under s 27, is left to regulations not retrieved.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Workplace Safety and Health Act 2006 -- duties, orders and penalties`

§§ `6(2), (3) -- who counts as an employee`

-- s 6(1): "employee" means a person employed "under a contract of service".
-- s 6(2): an employee includes "a volunteer who (a) does work for another person with the knowledge or consent of that other
-- person; and (b) does the work on an ongoing and regular basis", being work "in connection with any trade, business,
-- profession or undertaking carried on by that other person".
-- s 6(3): it includes "a person who is in a workplace for the purpose of receiving on the job training or gaining work
-- experience, including under a rehabilitation scheme".
DECLARE Worker HAS
    `has a contract of service` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a volunteer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `works with the other person's knowledge or consent` IS A BOOLEAN
    `works on an ongoing and regular basis` IS A BOOLEAN
    `work is connected with the other person's business` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is there for on the job training or work experience` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is an on-duty member of a listed uniformed service` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is crew on international transport posing no risk beyond the crew` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the worker` IS A Worker
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the worker is treated as an employee` IF
        `the worker`'s `has a contract of service`
    OR  (    `the worker`'s `is a volunteer`
         AND `the worker`'s `works with the other person's knowledge or consent`
         AND `the worker`'s `works on an ongoing and regular basis`
         AND `the worker`'s `work is connected with the other person's business`)
    OR  `the worker`'s `is there for on the job training or work experience`

§§ `62(1) and Sixth Schedule -- persons outside the Act`

-- s 62(1): "The provisions of this Act do not apply to the persons at work specified in the Sixth Schedule, even though their
-- work is carried out or performed in a workplace." Sixth Schedule: 1. members of the Police Force (including the Special
-- Constabulary), Prisons Service, Internal Security Department, Central Narcotics Bureau, Armed Forces, Civil Defence Force and
-- the Immigration & Checkpoints Authority "while on duty"; 2. a crew member on board a ship, aircraft or other international
-- mode of transport "which does not pose a risk to the safety and health of any person other than" the crew.
-- (Ministerial and Commissioner exemptions under s 62(2), (3) are not modelled.)
GIVEN `the worker` IS A Worker
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act does not apply to the worker` IF
        `the worker`'s `is an on-duty member of a listed uniformed service`
    OR  `the worker`'s `is crew on international transport posing no risk beyond the crew`

-- s 3(1): the Act "binds the Government"; (2) "Nothing in this Act renders the Government liable to prosecution for an offence";
-- (3) a person is not immune "by reason that the person is engaged to provide services to the Government".
GIVEN `is the Government` IS A BOOLEAN, `is a contractor to the Government` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `can be prosecuted under the Act` IF
    NOT `is the Government`

§§ `14(2), 14A(4) -- principals and contractors`

-- s 14(1): a principal must take reasonably practicable measures for the safety and health of its contractors, their
-- subcontractors and their employees "when at work". (2): "The duty ... only applies where the contractor, subcontractor or
-- employee ... is working under the direction of the principal as to the manner in which the work is carried out."
-- s 14A(1) (contractors engaged on or after 1 September 2011): the principal must ensure the contractor has the necessary
-- expertise and has taken adequate safety measures. (4): it is NOT a defence to rely "solely by providing ... by a term in a
-- contract with the principal's contractor, that the contractor has complied with or will comply".
GIVEN `working under the principal's direction as to manner` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the principal owes the section 14(1) duty` IF
    `working under the principal's direction as to manner`

GIVEN `proved reasonably practicable measures were taken` IS A BOOLEAN, `the measures were solely a contract term` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the principal has a defence under section 14A` IF
    `proved reasonably practicable measures were taken` AND (NOT `the measures were solely a contract term`)

§§ `18 -- protection of employees`

-- s 18(1): an employer must not deduct from wages, or receive any payment from an employee, "in respect of anything to be done
-- or provided by the employer in accordance with this Act in order to ensure the safety, health or welfare" of employees.
-- s 18(2): an employer "must not dismiss or threaten to dismiss an employee because the employee" (a) assisted an inspector or
-- other public authority in an inspection or investigation, (b) in good faith sought help from or reported to an inspector,
-- (c) performed duties in good faith as a member of a workplace safety and health committee, or (d) complied with a s 21
-- order or otherwise complied with the Act -- "or proposes to do so". (5): $5,000 or 6 months or both.
DECLARE Reason IS ONE OF
    `assisted an inspector's investigation`
    `reported a safety matter to an inspector in good faith`
    `acted in good faith as a safety committee member`
    `complied with a stop-work order`
    `proposed to report a safety matter to an inspector`
    `poor work performance`
    `redundancy`

GIVEN `the reason` IS A Reason
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the reason is protected` IF
    CONSIDER `the reason`
    WHEN `poor work performance` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `redundancy` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `dismissed or threatened to dismiss` IS A BOOLEAN, `the reason` IS A Reason
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the dismissal is an offence under section 18` IF
    `dismissed or threatened to dismiss` AND `the reason is protected` `the reason`

GIVEN `charged the employee for safety measures required by the Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the charge is an offence under section 18(1)` IF
    `charged the employee for safety measures required by the Act`

-- s 18(3), (4): the occupier must keep documents issued by the Commissioner, notices to the Commissioner and reports, "for at
-- least 5 years from the date the records were made or such other period as may be prescribed". (6): $2,000.
-- (Any other prescribed period is not modelled.)
GIVEN `years since the record was made` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the record must still be kept` IF
    `years since the record was made` LESS THAN 5

§§ `25 -- the scene of a fatal accident`

-- s 25(1): a person must not, without the Commissioner's consent, (a) alter, replace, remove or add to machinery, equipment, plant
-- or article "which may have contributed to the cause of any accident resulting in the death of any person or any dangerous
-- occurrence or occupational disease"; or (b) modify the scene. (3): the alteration is presumed to have been made by the
-- occupier "unless it is proved to the contrary". (4): nothing "operates to interfere with rescue work or work necessary for the
-- general safety of life and property". (5): $10,000 or 12 months or both.
DECLARE Incident IS ONE OF
    `a fatal accident`
    `a non-fatal accident`
    `a dangerous occurrence`
    `an occupational disease`

GIVEN `the incident` IS AN Incident, `altered the scene` IS A BOOLEAN, `the Commissioner consented` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the work was rescue or for the general safety of life and property` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `altering the scene is an offence under section 25` IF
        `altered the scene`
    AND (NOT `the Commissioner consented`)
    AND (NOT `the work was rescue or for the general safety of life and property`)
    AND (CONSIDER `the incident`
         WHEN `a non-fatal accident` THEN FALSE
         OTHERWISE TRUE)

§§ `21, 22 -- remedial and stop-work orders`

-- s 22(1): a person affected by a s 21 order "may, within 14 days of the date of service of the order, appeal to the Minister".
-- (2): where the appeal is against a remedial order, "that order does not have effect pending the outcome of the appeal".
-- (3): against a stop-work order, the person "must comply with the order pending the outcome of the appeal".
DECLARE Order IS ONE OF
    `a remedial order`
    `a stop-work order`

GIVEN `days since service` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the appeal is in time` IF
    `days since service` AT MOST 14

GIVEN `the order` IS AN Order, `an appeal is pending` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order has effect` IF
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `a stop-work order` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE NOT `an appeal is pending`

§§ `47, 48 -- the onus on the accused and on officers`

-- s 47: where the duty is to do something "so far as is reasonably practicable", "it is for the accused to prove" that it was not
-- reasonably practicable to do more, or that there was no better practicable means.
GIVEN `the accused proved nothing more was reasonably practicable` IS A BOOLEAN, `the duty was breached in fact` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the reasonably practicable duty is breached` IF
    `the duty was breached in fact` AND (NOT `the accused proved nothing more was reasonably practicable`)

-- s 48(1): where a body corporate commits an offence, "an officer of the body corporate shall be guilty of the offence ... unless
-- the officer proves that (a) the offence was committed without his or her consent or connivance; AND (b) he or she had
-- exercised all such diligence to prevent the commission of the offence as he or she ought". Officer includes any director,
-- partner, manager, secretary "and includes any person purporting to act in any such capacity" (s 48(5)).
GIVEN `the company committed an offence` IS A BOOLEAN, `is an officer` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `proved no consent or connivance` IS A BOOLEAN, `proved all due diligence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer is guilty under section 48` IF
        `the company committed an offence`
    AND `is an officer`
    AND (NOT (`proved no consent or connivance` AND `proved all due diligence`))

§§ `15, 18, 20, 21, 23, 25, 26, 46, 50, 51, 53 -- maximum penalties`

-- s 20: a contravention of any Part 4 provision imposing a duty is an offence. No penalty is expressly provided, so s 50 applies:
-- natural person $200,000 or 2 years or both; body corporate $500,000; continuing after conviction $2,000 / $5,000 a day.
-- s 15(3): a person at work who "without reasonable cause, wilfully or recklessly does any act which endangers" safety or health
-- "shall be guilty of an offence" -- no penalty stated, so s 50 applies too. s 15(4): a NEGLIGENT act -- $30,000 or 2 years or
-- both. s 15(5): misusing or not using protective equipment (s 15(1), (2)) -- $1,000; second or subsequent conviction $2,000.
-- s 18(5): $5,000 or 6 months or both. s 18(6) records: $2,000. s 21(6) remedial order: $50,000 or 12 months or both, plus $5,000
-- a day. s 21(7) stop-work order: $500,000 or 12 months or both, plus $20,000 a day. s 21(10) entering a secured area: $5,000 or
-- 6 months. s 23(3) working under a suspended certificate: $5,000 or 6 months. s 25(5): $10,000 or 12 months. s 26(7) obstructing
-- an Inquiry Committee: $2,000. s 46 unregistered factory: $5,000 or 6 months, plus $500 or 7 days a day. s 53 forgery and false
-- entries: $5,000 or 6 months.
-- s 51: a person previously convicted of an offence "that causes the death of any person" and "subsequently convicted of the same
-- offence that causes the death of another person" may be fined up to $400,000 (natural person) or $1 million (body corporate).
-- (INFERENCE: s 51 is read as replacing the fine maximum for any offence under the Act, not only s 50 offences; its text is not
-- limited to s 50.)
-- (The expressly stated imprisonment terms are returned whether or not the offender is a body corporate; the text does not say.)
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `breach of a Part 4 duty`
    `wilfully or recklessly endangering safety`
    `negligently endangering safety`
    `misusing or not using protective equipment`
    `penalising an employee or platform worker`
    `failing to keep workplace records`
    `failing to comply with a remedial order`
    `failing to comply with a stop-work order`
    `entering an area secured by the Commissioner`
    `working under a suspended certificate`
    `altering an accident scene`
    `obstructing an Inquiry Committee`
    `using an unregistered factory`
    `forging a certificate or making a false entry`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the general penalty applies` IF
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `breach of a Part 4 duty` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `wilfully or recklessly endangering safety` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence, `is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN, `is a second or subsequent conviction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the base maximum fine` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `breach of a Part 4 duty` THEN (IF `is a body corporate` THEN 500000 ELSE 200000)
    WHEN `wilfully or recklessly endangering safety` THEN (IF `is a body corporate` THEN 500000 ELSE 200000)
    WHEN `negligently endangering safety` THEN 30000
    WHEN `misusing or not using protective equipment` THEN (IF `is a second or subsequent conviction` THEN 2000 ELSE 1000)
    WHEN `penalising an employee or platform worker` THEN 5000
    WHEN `failing to keep workplace records` THEN 2000
    WHEN `failing to comply with a remedial order` THEN 50000
    WHEN `failing to comply with a stop-work order` THEN 500000
    WHEN `entering an area secured by the Commissioner` THEN 5000
    WHEN `working under a suspended certificate` THEN 5000
    WHEN `altering an accident scene` THEN 10000
    WHEN `obstructing an Inquiry Committee` THEN 2000
    WHEN `using an unregistered factory` THEN 5000
    OTHERWISE 5000

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence, `is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN, `is a second or subsequent conviction` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `is a repeat offence causing death` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine` MEANS
    IF `is a repeat offence causing death`
    THEN (IF `is a body corporate` THEN 1000000 ELSE 400000)
    ELSE `the base maximum fine` `the offence` `is a body corporate` `is a second or subsequent conviction`

-- s 50(b): a body corporate convicted of a general-penalty offence is liable "to a fine not exceeding $500,000" only.
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence, `is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `breach of a Part 4 duty` THEN (IF `is a body corporate` THEN 0 ELSE 24)
    WHEN `wilfully or recklessly endangering safety` THEN (IF `is a body corporate` THEN 0 ELSE 24)
    WHEN `negligently endangering safety` THEN 24
    WHEN `misusing or not using protective equipment` THEN 0
    WHEN `penalising an employee or platform worker` THEN 6
    WHEN `failing to keep workplace records` THEN 0
    WHEN `failing to comply with a remedial order` THEN 12
    WHEN `failing to comply with a stop-work order` THEN 12
    WHEN `entering an area secured by the Commissioner` THEN 6
    WHEN `working under a suspended certificate` THEN 6
    WHEN `altering an accident scene` THEN 12
    WHEN `obstructing an Inquiry Committee` THEN 0
    WHEN `using an unregistered factory` THEN 6
    OTHERWISE 6

-- Further fine for every day or part of a day the offence continues after conviction (ss 21(6), (7), 46(2)(b), 50(c), (d)).
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence, `is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum daily fine after conviction` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `breach of a Part 4 duty` THEN (IF `is a body corporate` THEN 5000 ELSE 2000)
    WHEN `wilfully or recklessly endangering safety` THEN (IF `is a body corporate` THEN 5000 ELSE 2000)
    WHEN `failing to comply with a remedial order` THEN 5000
    WHEN `failing to comply with a stop-work order` THEN 20000
    WHEN `using an unregistered factory` THEN 500
    OTHERWISE 0

§§ `56 -- composition`

-- s 56(1): the Commissioner may compound an offence "prescribed as a compoundable offence" by collecting "a sum not exceeding one
-- half of the amount of the maximum fine that is prescribed for the offence or a sum of $5,000, whichever is the lower".
-- (Which offences are prescribed as compoundable is in regulations not retrieved.)
GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lower of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

GIVEN `maximum fine` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum composition sum` MEANS
    `the lower of` (`maximum fine` DIVIDED BY 2) 5000

§§ `60 -- civil liability`

-- s 60(1): "Nothing in this Act is to be construed (a) as conferring a right of action in any civil proceedings in respect of any
-- contravention ... or (b) as conferring a defence to an action in any civil proceedings". (2): this does not affect the extent
-- (if any) to which a breach of a duty under "any written law" is actionable.
GIVEN `the provision was contravened` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act itself gives a right to sue` IF
    FALSE
